==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:bor|fr:disposer|text=+|tree=1}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dɪsˈpoʊz/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈspəʊz/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-dispose.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊz|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive|with ''[[of]]''}} To [[eliminate]] or to [[get rid of]] something.
#: {{ux|en|I '''dispose''' of my trash in the garbage can.}}
# To [[distribute]] or [[arrange]]; to [[put]] [[in place]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|4|III|text=Now, dear soldiers, march away: / And how thou pleasest, God, '''dispose''' the day!}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Sense and Sensibility|6|text=Marianne’s pianoforte was unpacked and properly '''disposed''' of, and Elinor’s drawing were affixed to the walls of their sitting rooms.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1934|author=w:Rex Stout|title=[[w:Fer-de-Lance (book)|Fer-de-Lance]]|year_published=1992|publisher=[[w:Bantam Books|Bantam]]|isbn=0553278193|page=47
|passage=I sat down within three feet of the entrance door, and I had no sooner got '''disposed''' than the door opened and a man came in{{...}}.}}
# To [[deal out]]; to [[assign]] to a [[use]].
#* '''1818''' (first published), {{w|John Evelyn}}, ''diary entry for 1634''
# To [[incline]].
#: {{ux|en|In these uncertain times, I am '''disposed''' towards caution.}} (Used here intransitively in the passive voice)
#* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|passage=Endure and conquer; Jove will soon '''dispose''' / To future good our past and present woes.}}
#* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Suspicion|passage=They [suspicions] '''dispose''' kings to tyranny, husbands to jealousy, and wise men to irresolution and melancholy.}}
#* {{RQ:Besant Ivory Gate|II
|passage=At twilight in the summer{{...}}the mice come out. They{{...}}eat the luncheon crumbs. Mr. Checkly, for instance, always brought his dinner in a paper parcel in his coat-tail pocket, and ate it when so '''disposed''', sprinkling crumbs lavishly{{...}}on the floor.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[bargain]]; to [[make]] [[term]]s.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|act=I|scene=ii|passage=She had '''disposed''' with Caesar.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[regulate]]; to [[adjust]]; to [[settle]]; to [[determine]].
#* {{RQ:Dryden Fables|chapter=Palamon and Arcite|passage=the knightly forms of combat to '''dispose'''}}

